Yes. Once the world-model gets rich enough, central control becomes the wrong abstraction.

If you try to hold every resolution, every delta, every capability, every person, every update, every uncertainty, and every future branch in one command loop, you create a bottleneck.

The stronger architecture is hierarchical and event-driven:

world
→ many local models at different resolutions
→ each tracks its own deltas
→ only meaningful changes propagate upward
→ higher layers maintain invariants / goals
→ lower layers retain autonomy

So instead of:

control everything

you use:

coordinate what matters, let the rest self-regulate.

A good stack might be:

raw observations
→ local state estimators
→ delta detectors
→ capability registry
→ relevance / priority gate
→ shared world model
→ goal / invariant layer
→ action
→ AAR

And each resolution has its own clock.

Some variables update milliseconds apart.
Some daily.
Some yearly.
Some only when a regime changes.

Trying to recompute all of them at one cadence wastes enormous effort.

So you get asynchronous reality:

fast loops handle immediate control
medium loops handle adaptation
slow loops handle strategy / identity / doctrine

Then add exception routing:

nothing meaningful changed
→ do nothing

local change
→ update locally

cross-domain change
→ notify related models

invariant threatened
→ escalate

new capability discovered
→ register it + expose it where relevant

capability unused / forgotten
→ resurface it contextually rather than constantly

That last part matters. You don’t need every capability mentally “loaded” at all times. You need retrievability.

Like virtual memory:

capability exists
≠
capability must occupy working memory

So maybe the core Pantheon architecture becomes:

Distributed Coherence

local autonomy
+
shared invariants
+
delta-driven communication
+
retrievable capability memory
+
periodic synchronization

And your final question is the deepest one:

Why control it all?

You usually don’t need to.

Control is expensive.

Often what you actually need is:

visibility
coordination
bounded autonomy
error correction
recoverability
ability to intervene when necessary

That is closer to how bodies, ecosystems, militaries, markets, brains, and distributed software already work.

The brain does not consciously control each heartbeat, muscle fiber, immune cell, and sensory receptor. It delegates enormous amounts and intervenes only where higher-order coordination matters.

So:

Don’t build a king that moves every piece.
Build a kingdom that knows when the king actually needs to hear about something.

That gives you scale without drowning in your own model.